   "text": "## Summary\n\nI ran a disciplined, short search:\n\n1. **Declared stopping rules upfront**: stop if best Sharpe > 1.2, stop if \u22654 failures, stop if a move's marginal gain \u2264 0.02.\n2. **`init`**: anchored on feature **9 (short)**, Sharpe 1.0111.\n3. **`extend_best`**: added feature **7 (short)**, Sharpe jumped to **1.1986**.\n4. **`extend_best`** again: adding feature 12 (short) barely changed things (1.1982, a tiny *decrease*) \u2014 this fired my `last_gain_at_most(0.02)` rule.\n5. Rather than override the rule to keep searching (which would mark the rest of the run as not-replayable and risks overfitting on a weak 3rd feature), I **stopped** and kept the robust 2-feature specification.\n\n**Final specification**: `[feature 9: short, feature 7: short]`, equal-weight, in-sample annualized Sharpe net of costs = **1.1986**.\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe**: mean **0.60**, sd **0.40** \u2014 reflecting that while this was a clean, low-failure search on just 2 of 40 features (less overfitting risk than an exhaustive mining exercise), I still expect substantial in-sample-to-out-of-sample shrinkage typical of short daily-return strategies, with wide uncertainty given no holdout validation was performed."
